Your chances of finding something that is there, are at worst 1/7-
that means that the chances of not finding it while searching
100 times are about 1 in 5,000,000.. Also, be aware the searching 
searches the squares around you, so just being in the room is not enough.
Heres some tips on picking where to search:
  1) Passage ways that just suddenly end are suspicious, try search at
     the end of them, then working back searching as you go.
  2) Remember that the map is limited to the screen- if you are in a
     room on the far right side of the screen, you are less likely to
     find a door (leading anywhere new) on the right than on the left. 
  3) Shops are usually (but not always) near the edge of the screen-
     they also need some space, so look for regions of the map
     you havent found anything in yet.
  4) If you are searching in a room, remember that you get some overlap
     in your search, because adjacent includes diagonal, i.e.
     
     if you search 3 times here:
         --------
         |     @|
         |      |
     then search 3 times here:
         --------
         |    @ |
         |      |
     then search 3 times here:
         --------
         |   @  |
         |      |
     the square with the +
         -----+--
         |   @  |
         |      |     
     got searched 9 times, and you covered a lot more area than if you stayed
     in one spot and searched 9 times (after 9 times, you are at least
     75% likely to have found something secret). So if you search 3 times
     and move to the next square, each of the spots will get searched 9
     times (if 75% isnt good enough, then you could do 4 on each square,
     totalling 12 searches, which brings you up to aobut 85%)

